+## 1. Executive verdict
+
+**Verdict: FOUNDATION EXISTS, 2,501 MEMBERSHIP IS NOT READY TO INGEST.** There is substantial reusable architecture: one canonical Kanji table, component/radical records, decomposition edges, KANJIDIC2/JMdict/KanjiVG contracts, reading and lexical services, a 100-character source-backed MindTree slice, SVG parsers/renderers, frontend pages, and controlled provisioning planning. The committed canonical first-party foundation is still a 33-Kanji Mind Tree plus a separate 12-Kanji corpus pilot—not a complete 2,501-character implementation. The source-backed static visual/facts slice has 100 characters; the full source files referenced by several ingestion/renderer tests are intentionally absent from this checkout.
+
+**The reported provenance defect was present at HEAD and is corrected in this working tree.** `first-party:kanji-corpus:v1` was an alias for upstream KANJIDIC2 2024-07 and lacked an explicit first-party registry entry. The correction registers it as first party, removes that alias, labels the ETL source row as first party, and makes the ETL source registration fail closed on conflicting pre-existing metadata. Four new DB-free tests establish the 12/33 provenance separation. This correction does **not** mutate or repair an already persisted `knowledge_sources` row; an old conflicting row requires a separately authorized, read-only investigation followed by its own approval.
+
+**PR #27 is still open and is not part of this checkout.** Its CI is green, but it covers Drizzle ETL loader/pipeline surfaces only. It does not establish a universal database write gate. Raw PostgreSQL scripts remain separate writers. Database roles/grants—not accountability flags—are the actual database authorization boundary. No claim that all writers are blocked is justified.
+
+**Next bounded implementation step:** establish and review the exact 2,501-character selection policy and create a pinned, deduplicated membership manifest only. Do not start bulk Kanji generation or ingestion until that membership and the source/license contract have explicit approval.
