+
+### 5.1 PR #27’s actual scope
+
+- **[Verified—PR #27 description/files]** PR #27 adds an input-free denial guard to the Drizzle ETL Kanji surfaces: `ensureKanjiSource`, `loadKanjiRadicals`, `loadKanjiEntries`, `loadKanjiComposition`, `loadTransformedKanji`, and `runKanjiETLPipeline`. It is intended to deny shared ETL write calls before database access, transaction acquisition, input traversal or result mutation. PR description documents database-free tests and a fail-closed test executor.
+- **[Verified—checkout]** The checked-out `src/etl/kanji/loader.ts` and `src/etl/kanji/pipeline.ts` do not contain `canonicalWriteGate` or `denyCanonicalKanjiWrite`; the guard file is absent. Therefore the PR gate is **not yet in HEAD**, regardless of green PR checks.
+- PR #27's gate is a code-level denial of the covered Drizzle loader/pipeline paths. It is not a universal PostgreSQL write firewall and is not evidence that unrelated scripts, database credentials, migrations, or runtime roles are gated.
+
+### 5.2 Separate writer surfaces
+
+| Surface | Mechanism and actual scope | Security reading |
+|---|---|---|
+| Drizzle ETL loader/pipeline (`src/etl/kanji/loader.ts`, `pipeline.ts`) | Covered by the proposed PR #27 fail-closed gate; not covered on this checkout's HEAD. | Do not describe as protected in the checked-out branch until PR #27 is merged and verified there. Even then, claim only the named exports/surface. |
+| Raw KANJIDIC2 writer (`scripts/ingest-kanjidic2.ts`) | Opens its own `pg.Client`, starts its own transaction and writes `kanji_entries`. Supports a `--from-baseline` path with `DELETE FROM kanji_entries WHERE source_ref = $1`. It does not import the Drizzle ETL gate. | **Not covered by PR #27.** It has separate source/hash/count checks, target checks, baseline expectations and transaction handling, but an environment-declared disposable target is not database authentication. This script was not run. |
+| Controlled corpus operator CLI (`scripts/provision-kanji-corpus.ts`) | A raw PostgreSQL connection uses `verify`, `dry-run`, and `execute`; execute has target confirmation, operator/approval fields, an advisory lock, one transaction, insert-only plan, invariant checks and rollback. It provisions the curated first-party baseline—not arbitrary source ingestion. | **Not covered by the ETL denial gate.** Its own protections should be preserved. CLI flags record intent/accountability; they are not credentials and do not grant access. This CLI was not run. |
+| Other raw PostgreSQL code | Must be assessed individually; `scripts/ingest-kanji-jmdict-graph.ts` is a separate derived-graph publishing writer with its own baseline/state/target checks. | Not covered merely because it concerns Kanji or shares a repo. The local test attempting its DB path failed closed because local PostgreSQL was unavailable. No direct script was invoked. |
+| Database authorization | PostgreSQL role privileges, ownership, RLS/trigger policy and credential scope are enforced by the database/provider. The provisioning runbook specifies least privilege (SELECT/INSERT on the three canonical tables; no UPDATE/DELETE/TRUNCATE/DDL) for the operator role. | **This is the actual authorization boundary.** The session did not inspect provider grants or verify deployed roles. A runbook is not proof that grants are configured. |
+| Accountability/confirmation parameters | `--operator`, `--approval-ref`, `--accept-target-class`, `--confirm-target`, plus environment target markers used by scripts. | Useful audit/intent controls only. They are not authentication, authorization, or proof that a target is disposable. Do not call them a security boundary. |
