+## 8. Minimum coherent target data model (recommendation)
+
+Reuse existing canonical identity, provenance registry, dictionary, component registry, and visual service. Do not add a second Kanji master, duplicate vocabulary table, or stroke table by default.
+
+1. **Stable identity:** continue unique Unicode Kanji `character` plus stable `kanji_entries.id`; retain source glyph/variant identities as separate variant evidence. Keep NFC canonical identity; do not compatibility-collapse characters.
+2. **Core Kanji facts:** keep canonical display/query fields in `kanji_entries`, but add an explicit, non-lossy representation for KANJIDIC2 meanings and metadata where needed. Recommended additive `kanji_readings` relation: `kanji_id`, reading kind (`on`, `kun`, `nanori`), exact source string, normalized search form, source annotations/restrictions, source record reference and source release. Existing JSON arrays can remain as API-compatible projections during migration. Do not infer a missing reading from words.
+3. **Field provenance:** keep `knowledge_sources` as source/release registry and row `sourceRef` for coarse ownership. Because today’s Kanji row gives one `sourceRef` for multiple independently sourced fields and edges have none, add a narrowly scoped evidence relation (e.g. `kanji_field_evidence`) only when ingestion starts: entity/field key, source ID, source record key, raw/value hash or immutable artifact pointer, normalization version and observed value status. Keep editorial claim/review as a separate overlay, not a replacement source fact. This is justified by required per-field attribution and reconciliation; do not turn the source registry into duplicated field data.
+4. **Components and decompositions:** retain `kanji_radicals` as a component identity registry (despite legacy table name), preserving radical vs non-radical kind and visual variants. Add a decomposition-set identity only when expanding reviewed decompositions: `kanji_decomposition_sets` groups one valid model/interpretation for one Kanji and includes method/relationship kind, source, review status and reviewer evidence. Add a set reference to `kanji_composition`; optionally edge-level provenance when edge facts can differ from set source. Preserve alternate sets; one selected default is UI policy, not deletion of alternatives. Keep KanjiVG structural groups in the source-evidence artifact and label them visual. Active traversal should be bounded and cycle-checked; candidate conflicts can be quarantined instead of coerced into a DAG.
+5. **Dictionary:** reuse `dictionary_entries`, source registry and `example_sentences`; verify retention of JMdict’s written forms, reading associations/restrictions and sense/POS/gloss semantics. If real source preservation cannot fit current row JSON, use normalized child tables only after importer gap is demonstrated. Index `kanji_characters` with GIN only after query-plan tests. Derived Kanji-word links remain reproducible from the canonical lexicon and versioned algorithm; if persisted, store algorithm/version and link evidence, not as editorial fact.
+6. **Stroke assets:** no new DB table. Keep immutable, source-versioned SVG geometry/artifact files with source path hash and transformer version. Build deterministic animation SVG from vetted stroke paths. GIF is generated compatibility/export output with separate attribution and reproducibility metadata.
+7. **Editorial/review states:** extend/reuse existing CMS lifecycle concepts only where they apply; record draft, review-pending, approved, published and rejected distinctly, with reviewer identity/time and source-evidence references. Do not mark generated data reviewed.
+
+Schema changes above are future recommendations, not K0 edits. Existing `kanji_entries`, `kanji_radicals`, `kanji_composition`, `dictionary_entries`, `knowledge_sources`, CMS lifecycle and static artifact patterns should be reused. Introduce each table/column only with an importer/API requirement and a migration/test proving that requirement.
+
+## 9. Graph architecture recommendation
+
+Use two explicitly separate graphs:
+
+- **Visual source graph:** KanjiVG file → SVG group occurrences → ordered stroke paths and literal source annotations. Identity includes pinned release, source filename and file SHA-256; transformation versioned. It states what the source SVG encodes, not why the Kanji historically developed.
+- **Learning decomposition graph:** canonical Kanji/component identities → one or more grouped decomposition sets → edges with semantic/phonetic/positional/structural relation, rendered form, order, source/evidence, editorial status and reviewer evidence. Components can be radicals, visual-only forms, primitives or whole Kanji; they are not all radicals. Alternative analyses are first-class, not overwrites.
+
+Validation must check identity uniqueness, known endpoints, normalized rendered forms, order uniqueness per set, source/evidence presence, self-edge policy, traversal bounds and cycles for learner-selected decompositions. Cyclic/ambiguous candidate structures remain review/quarantine findings instead of being silently “fixed.” Display labels must explicitly distinguish visual resemblance, source annotation, learning decomposition and etymological research.
+
+## 10. JMdict integration recommendation
+
+Keep JMdict entries canonical in the existing dictionary store. Preserve written elements and their restrictions, reading elements and their restrictions, sense boundaries, POS, glosses, information/dialect/field tags and the pinned source identity. A Kanji-to-vocabulary link is a deterministic relation derived from a written form’s literal characters; a reading-type classification is a separate algorithmic annotation with a version and confidence/explanation, not source truth. Do not derive a character reading by slicing the entire word reading unless a validated alignment exists. Retain vocabulary-level examples through existing dictionary/example-sentence paths; do not copy entry meanings into Kanji as if they were Kanji senses.
+
+Before scale-up, prove exact written-form/readings/senses equivalence for source samples including kana-only headwords, multiple written forms, reading restrictions, multiple readings, POS/sense boundaries, irregular/ateji words, and non-English translation license exclusions.
+
+## 11. KanjiVG animation architecture recommendation
+
+Treat original SVG/path data as immutable source evidence. Sanitize once during deterministic normalization; verify one character, expected viewBox, permitted elements/attributes, path data bounds, ordered source IDs and source file digest. Record stroke count independently from KANJIDIC2 and emit discrepancies.
+
+For browser playback, prefer accessible inline SVG generated from the validated path list (not the entire upstream SVG), with a `<title>`, visible stroke number/status, play/replay/pause or step control, keyboard access, `prefers-reduced-motion` support, and a static fallback. Use measured path lengths or `pathLength` normalization rather than assuming the same dash length for every stroke. Missing/invalid source SVG maps to an explicit unavailable/quarantined state, never a fake stroke. Keep response sizes bounded and cache by canonical character + source/transform version.
+
+GIF generation is optional and should be a reproducible export path, not a requirement for browser use. It adds rasterized storage, build tooling, CPU, accessibility limitations and another derivative subject to upstream attribution/share-alike obligations.
+
+## 12. Deterministic batch ingestion and acceptance plan
+
+### 12.1 Batch identity and repeatability
+
+- Freeze the 2,501-row corpus manifest and SHA-256 before splitting. Suggested batch identity: `kanji-2501-batch-0001`; record manifest digest, exact ordinal/character range or explicit member hash, source release IDs and hashes, parser/normalizer versions, graph ruleset version, and expected output hashes.
+- Default to approximately 50 Kanji per delivery batch. Batch size may be reduced for complex/ambiguous records or increased only if review capacity supports it. Corpus membership/order never changes because components are reviewed together.
+- Pin source release bytes and license/attribution notices for KANJIDIC2, JMdict and KanjiVG. Source refresh is a new versioned derivation, not an in-place update.
+- Normalize deterministically: Unicode scalar/NFC policy, stable ordering, explicit null/unknown/not-applicable/review-pending state, stable IDs, parsed source restrictions and exact source record IDs. Do not invent missing content.
+- Duplicate detection: exact code point, normalized character key, source-record ID, stable entity ID, duplicate written/reading pair, equivalent edge identity and conflicting content hash. Quarantine conflicts; never last-write-wins.
+- Separate immutable raw/source facts, derived indices/links, and first-party editorial overlays. Record per-field evidence and transform version.
+
+### 12.2 Dry run, import, idempotency and rollback
+
+1. **Preflight:** verify repository/source/license pins and target DB schema version; confirm target authorization using DB role grants. Target-class and operator flags do not authenticate.
+2. **Dry-run plan:** read-only snapshot; calculate exact inserts/no-ops/conflicts/quarantine, FK/reference gaps, changed hashes, missing KanjiVG assets and expected before/after counts. Persist a canonical, reviewable plan digest; perform zero writes.
+3. **Review:** independent review of all 50 character identities and source conflicts; Japanese-language review of meanings/readings and nontrivial graph interpretations; approve source/license, editorial overlay and missing-state classifications separately.
+4. **Execute:** operator CLI only, least-privilege role, explicit approval record, single transaction per batch, advisory lock, re-plan under lock, no destructive updates/deletes, constraints/invariants, audit record. Abort on drift. Do not provide a generic production bypass.
+5. **Idempotency:** same pins + manifest + transformation version + database state yields the same plan/digests; rerun must show zero inserts/updates and zero drift. Any content hash difference is a conflict requiring a new reviewed version.
+6. **Rollback:** transaction rollback on every invariant failure; retain the previous immutable artifact version. Prove rollback on a disposable database before batch approval. Do not use production writes to test rollback.
+7. **Quarantine:** store/report invalid source records, disagreements, missing variants, illegal/license uncertainty and graph conflicts with reason codes and evidence. Unknown remains unknown.
+8. **Batch report:** include identity/hash, source releases/licenses, record counts, inserts/no-ops/conflicts/quarantine, missing asset and stroke discrepancy counts, graph metrics, dictionary-link coverage, validation outputs, reviewer/approver evidence, and final digest.
+
+### 12.3 Automated batch acceptance (all required)
+
+- Exactly the manifest members for that batch; no duplicates; no membership drift; canonical IDs deterministic.
+- Source hashes/version/license allowlist pass; every imported field has source evidence or explicit first-party/editorial ownership; provenance registry resolves all references without alias ambiguity.
+- Readings preserve On/Kun/Nanori distinctions, dots/okurigana, source restrictions and not-available states; no unsupported reading/meaning/grade/stroke/JLPT values fabricated.
+- Decomposition sets have valid references, stable unique edges/order, explicit relation/source type, no orphan nodes; any active learner tree passes bounded traversal/cycle checks. Visual KanjiVG annotations are not promoted to semantic/phonetic truths.
+- JMdict linkages reference existing dictionary IDs and written-form character membership; preserve sense/POS/restriction boundaries; deterministic reverse index equals forward derivation; report unlinked and ambiguous cases.
+- KanjiVG assets pass source hash, SVG security and path-order validation; every missing/invalid asset appears in a machine-readable report and UI fallback; source-vs-KANJIDIC2 stroke discrepancies do not overwrite either source value.
+- Dry-run does zero writes; controlled disposable integration proves transaction, injected-failure rollback, untouched-row digest equality and rerun idempotency. Migrations run only in disposable test DB; never as part of this K0.
+- `npm run typecheck`, focused/full selected Vitest suites, `npm run lint`, production build, API contract tests and browser/mobile accessibility checks pass for touched behavior. No unexplained test failures.
+
+### 12.4 Manual batch acceptance (all required)
+
+- A Japanese-language reviewer checks all 50 readings/meanings against pinned source records and verifies source restrictions/notations; second review required for conflicts, ambiguity and editorial explanations.
+- A Mind Tree reviewer confirms component identity, role and position are pedagogical claims supported by cited evidence; source visual grouping and historical etymology are explicitly distinguished.
+- Licensing/attribution owner approves exact source versions and output distribution obligations before importing/distributing derivatives.
+- Product/accessibility review checks 10% sample minimum plus all exceptions on mobile and desktop, keyboard/screen reader labels, reduced motion, touch targets, loading/error/unavailable behavior, and no false “complete” states.
+- Named approver signs the batch report and exact plan digest. Rejection returns only affected records to quarantine; it never changes manifest membership without a separately reviewed manifest version.
+
+### 12.5 Shared-component review without manifest drift
+
+Build a review queue/index over component identity and count/centrality of references across the frozen corpus. Reviewers may see all records sharing a component (and related source evidence) together, but each Kanji retains its immutable manifest identity, batch assignment and acceptance status. A component cluster is a review/work-queue grouping only; it does not select, add, remove, reorder or reassign corpus members.
