+## 6. Membership and Unicode identity rules
+
+### 6.1 Proposed default model (awaiting owner approval)
+
+A member is one **exact Unicode scalar value** selected under the approved corpus policy and classified as an eligible Japanese-use Kanji identity. The membership manifest stores the literal scalar and its numeric code point as the primary identity; a deterministic internal ID derives from the code point, not from meaning, reading, glyph rendering or source row ID.
+
+This is a recommendation, not the final answer to which ideograph ranges are eligible. Unicode defines code points and properties; it does not decide the educational corpus.
+
+### 6.2 Distinctions that must remain explicit
+
+- **Distinct Unicode identity:** different scalar values remain different membership candidates even when they render similarly or are related historically. They are not silently merged.
+- **Character variant (linguistic/orthographic):** forms such as shinjitai and kyūjitai may have distinct code points and a relevant relationship. Whether either or both are selected is policy-driven; store a relation, not an alias rewrite.
+- **Glyph variant:** a font/design or KanjiVG variant drawing of the same code point is a visual rendering, not a second member. Store with asset/source metadata where needed.
+- **Compatibility ideograph:** a separately encoded compatibility code point may have a Unicode compatibility mapping to a unified ideograph. It remains a distinct code-point identity in a lossless manifest unless owner policy explicitly excludes it; map it to a related/canonical candidate separately. NFKC must not overwrite its identity.
+- **Unicode variation sequence:** base scalar plus variation selector requests a glyph presentation. Recommended default is one base-character membership identity, with the sequence stored as presentation metadata; owner must decide if the product’s target is sequences rather than characters.
+- **Traditional/simplified Chinese forms:** distinct characters or variants must not be assumed to have one-to-one Japanese mappings. Store the exact source relation and Japanese-use evidence. Do not include a form only because a Chinese conversion table pairs it with a Japanese form.
+- **Historical/rare/name Kanji:** identity alone does not establish corpus eligibility; record source, rationale and category. Jinmeiyō and Jōyō are related but distinct policy sources.
+- **JIS levels:** useful as encoding coverage metadata; not a synonym for Jōyō, JLPT or learner importance.
+
+### 6.3 Unicode normalization and duplicates
+
+`src/lib/japanese/kanjiText.ts` documents that NFC maps some compatibility ideographs to unified values (examples U+F900→U+8C48 and U+FA10→U+585A), while M1 structural evidence preserves exact strings and the ETL path can disable normalization. This makes normalization choice material to membership counts.
+
+**Recommended invariant:** duplicate detection for the membership count is by exact scalar identity after validating one scalar per entry—not by glyph similarity, reading, compatibility mapping, NFKC or variant relation. A separate normalization/canonical-link audit may report possible aliases but may not collapse them. If owner selects a normalized identity class instead, define it before generating records and retain original scalar values.
+
+### 6.4 Source coverage is not membership
+
+A member may be missing from KANJIDIC2, JMdict, or KanjiVG. Track per-source status separately, e.g. `present`, `missing`, `not_applicable`, `not_reviewed`, `quarantined`. Do not pad coverage, synthesize a stroke count, or remove a valid member to make source-coverage totals look complete.
+
+## 7. Proposed immutable manifest schema
+
+This describes data shape only. It is **not** the manifest and contains no approved membership.
+
+### 7.1 Required top-level fields
+
+- `schemaVersion`: version of manifest schema.
+- `manifestId`: stable corpus identity, e.g. owner-assigned name.
+- `manifestVersion`: immutable version; changed membership or policy requires a new version.
+- `targetCount`: exactly `2501` after approval.
+- `selectionPolicy`: ID, version, policy digest and methodology reference.
+- `identityPolicy`: Unicode version, eligible identity class, normalization and variant rules.
+- `sources`: membership and supporting-source entries with exact release/version, acquisition reference, artifact checksum/size, license identifier/notice, attribution and intended use.
+- `entries`: ordered, unique identity records.
+- `created`: fixed UTC creation time, creator and tool/version; timestamps are recorded once, not generated on every build.
+- `approvalStatus`: draft/review/approved/frozen.
+- `manifestSha256`: digest of the canonical document excluding the digest field itself.
+- `canonicalizationVersion`: stable rules for serialization and digest verification.
+
+### 7.2 Entry fields
+
+Each entry should include:
+
+- `ordinal` (unique deterministic order index).
+- `character` (exact scalar literal).
+- `codePoint` (uppercase `U+` form; cross-check by decoding the literal).
+- `canonicalIdentity` (stable code-point-derived internal identity).
+- `membership`: category, rationale code/text, membership source ID/release and source record/key. Multiple sources may be present; distinguish direct membership evidence from metadata evidence.
+- `variantRelationships`: zero or more typed relationships (`compatibility_mapping`, `shinjitai_of`, `kyujitai_of`, `traditional_variant_of`, `simplified_variant_of`, `glyph_sequence`, etc.), each with source, direction and review status. Relation vocabulary itself requires policy review.
+- `baselineReconciliation`: `mindtree_33`, `pilot_12`, `both`, `neither`; identity conflict and source-conflict flags; `included`, `outside_policy_preserved`, `duplicate_identity`, `pending_review` or `quarantined` status. Do not encode reconciliation as permission to delete.
+- `validationStatus`: e.g. `pending`, `valid`, `blocked`, `quarantined`; reason codes and evidence links.
+- `approvalStatus`: per-entry review status, reviewer-role reference, review evidence and timestamp; approval cannot be inferred from `valid` or from target count.
+
