+### 7.4 Serialization, immutability and hashing
+
+**Recommendation:** canonical JSON encoded as UTF-8 without BOM, LF line endings, fixed object-key ordering (e.g. a pinned RFC 8785/JCS implementation or a repository-owned versioned canonicalizer), no floating-point values, and entries sorted by numeric code point or an explicitly approved ordinal. Do not sort by locale-dependent collation. Do not normalize `character` during serialization.
+
+- Hash the exact canonical bytes of the manifest with SHA-256 after removing only the `manifestSha256` property. Include schema/policy version, sources and checksums, all entries, status and fixed creation metadata. Record the canonicalization version.
+- Approval signatures/decision evidence may be detached records referencing the final hash to avoid circular hashing; if the approval status field changes, the manifest body/hash changes and must be re-approved/frozen at the new digest.
+- Verify `character` is exactly one Unicode scalar and equals `codePoint`; verify unique scalar and canonical ID; reject duplicates, malformed code points, unresolved source IDs and invalid digest syntax.
+- After approval, fields affecting membership, identity, rationale, source pin, policy or approval are immutable. Corrections require a new `manifestVersion`, new digest, explicit supersedes reference, and machine-generated old→new membership/metadata delta report. Never amend a frozen file in place.
+- Batch reports must identify `manifestId`, `manifestVersion`, `manifestSha256`, exact member IDs/hash, source artifact hashes, transformation versions and approval evidence. This keeps old results reproducible after a future revision.
