+## 9. Reconciliation with existing 33 + 12 baseline
+
+Treat corpus membership as a policy layer separate from canonical records, source facts and UI support.
+
+1. **First reconcile exact identity:** compare code points, not just displayed glyphs or local string normalization, across the 33 Mind Tree records, 12 pilot records, 100 static artifacts, and eventual candidate list.
+2. **Do not delete:** if an existing record is not selected by approved policy, keep the record, source, history and edges intact; mark it `outside_policy_preserved` in a separate reconciliation report. The 2,501 count applies to the approved manifest only, not to a forced rewrite of legacy rows.
+3. **Duplicates:** same exact scalar represented by different source IDs is one membership identity with multiple source/evidence records; preserve both provenance histories and report the collision. Distinct code points with compatibility/variant relations remain distinct unless the approved identity policy explicitly excludes a class; do not merge to achieve 2,501.
+4. **Conflicting provenance:** block the affected record and source batch; no alias-based fallback, automatic “best source” selection, or overwrite. The preceding K0 correction ensures the corpus pilot ID no longer aliases KANJIDIC2; its tests are retained.
+5. **Missing KANJIDIC2/JMdict/KanjiVG coverage:** distinguish membership from data coverage. Use explicit missing/quarantine states; never fabricate readings, meanings, strokes, or source IDs. A missing stroke asset does not automatically invalidate membership.
+6. **Preserve data strata:** 55 existing Mind Tree elements, 8 normalized net-new pilot elements, 71 Mind Tree edges and 19 pilot edges are related-content inventory, not membership count. Component presence must not cause a Kanji to enter/leave the manifest.
+7. **100-character static slice:** use its 33 + 67 identity set and source hashes as a reconciliation sample only. It is a selection produced by a different policy (existing Mind Tree + grade-1 + available KanjiVG), not the first 100 entries or the canonical prefix of a future 2,501 set unless owner explicitly approves that policy.
+8. **Reports:** publish before/after membership deltas, duplicate/variant groups, baseline status, and source-coverage gaps. Any exception requires a reason and approver; exception rows do not silently change the fixed target count.
+
+All counts above are repository-model or historical reports. No live production database was contacted.
+
+## 10. Approval and change control
+
+### Workflow
+
+`DRAFT → REVIEW → APPROVED → FROZEN`
+
+- **DRAFT:** owner-approved policy exists in a document; candidate manifest is generated only from pinned sources. Membership rows carry no approval claim.
+- **REVIEW:** independent reviewers receive canonical manifest bytes, full-set count/dedup report, selection rationale, source coverage, license notices, baseline reconciliation, variant map, and machine-readable diff. Japanese/curriculum review samples do not substitute for an exact full-set identity check.
+- **APPROVED:** designated owner/curriculum approver approves purpose and policy; Japanese-language reviewer approves linguistic/source exception rationale; provenance/licensing reviewer approves pins/obligations; software/data reviewer verifies validators and deterministic digest. Record identities, roles, timestamps, decisions and digest. Legal-review role should be qualified where rights are uncertain.
+- **FROZEN:** publish read-only manifest file, checksum, detached approval record, source lock and validation report. Batch tooling may consume only frozen manifest version/hash.
+
+### Changes after freezing
+
+- No in-place edit. A change proposal states reason, old/new code points/metadata, effects on prior batches and downstream pages, source/release changes, and approval requirements.
+- Generate a new manifest version and digest with `supersedes: {manifestId, manifestVersion, sha256}` plus a deterministic delta (`added`, `removed`, `identity-policy changes`, `metadata-only changes`). A source refresh that does not change membership still gets new source/derivation artifact versions; whether the membership manifest itself changes depends on which source/policy facts it pins.
+- Never erase historical manifests or batch acceptance reports. Batch identity must include its exact parent manifest version+SHA and source/transform hashes, so later batches do not make old output irreproducible.
+- If a revised policy changes the count or identity class, obtain a new explicit owner approval. Do not preserve the string “2,501” by dropping, folding or substituting records without documenting that policy change.
+
+## 11. Risks and unresolved questions
+
+1. **Purpose ambiguity:** “2,501” does not reveal whether the owner means KANJIDIC2 newspaper frequency, a learner curriculum, a list from an earlier product plan, or a custom hybrid.
+2. **Rank coincidence:** repository history records 2,501 KANJIDIC2 frequency-tagged entries. That may be relevant, but no approved interpretation, membership list or exact distinct-rank validation exists here.
+3. **Standards-source gap:** Jōyō list and license pin are absent, despite historical reports quoting its count.
+4. **JLPT authority gap:** level fields exist, but membership cannot be inferred from those fields without an independently approved exhaustive list and edition.
+5. **Unicode mismatch risk:** NFC/NFKC search logic can fold compatibility values; exact identity contract and product selection must be aligned without destructive normalization.
+6. **Variant relations are many-to-many/contextual:** shinjitai/kyūjitai and simplified/traditional relations should not be assumed universal synonyms.
+7. **Source coverage bias:** requiring KanjiVG, KANJIDIC2, JMdict, JIS, grade or frequency availability for membership can systematically exclude categories a literacy corpus may need.
+8. **License blocker:** KANJIDIC2 historical 3.0 contract vs 4.0 notice must be adjudicated before further distribution of derived data.
+9. **Pilot provenance/linguistic quality:** the first-party corpus fixture header lists upstream categories broadly but does not field-cite them; the source ID is first-party. Keep this discrepancy visible and do not infer per-field upstream support.
+10. **Compatibility and source-update change:** Unicode and upstream standard releases can change mappings/classifications; pin Unicode version and all source revisions, then version changes instead of silently remapping identities.
+
+## 12. Go/no-go criteria for creating the actual manifest
+
+### GO only if all are true
+
+- Owner has signed a written selection policy and exact target identity/cardinality rules.
+- Strategy, purpose, source editions, extension quotas/tie-breaks and category definitions are explicit.
+- Exact Unicode identity, compatibility, traditional/simplified, shinjitai/kyūjitai, variation sequence, personal-name/historical and supplementary-plane scope is documented.
+- Membership sources are pinned to immutable bytes/releases with SHA-256, acquisition method, attribution and rights review. KANJIDIC2 license discrepancy is adjudicated for every derived field to be distributed.
+- Baseline treatment of all 45 first-party records is explicit; conflict/out-of-policy handling preserves records and provenance.
+- Manifest schema/canonicalization/hash convention and independent reviewers are approved.
+- A source-backed candidate build can be performed with deterministic ordering, exact deduplication and quarantine; no missing membership is inferred to fill the count.
+
+### NO-GO if any are true
+
+- The only rationale is that a report mentions “2,501 frequency-tagged entries.”
+- The policy is called “Jōyō+extension,” “JLPT,” “frequency,” or “hybrid” without source/edition/quota/tie-break definitions.
+- A member is added because its KanjiVG file exists, or excluded solely because an asset is missing, without owner-approved scope.
+- NFKC/visual similarity silently merges distinct code points.
+- The corpus reaches 2,501 only by deleting/overwriting existing records or omitting conflicts without a report.
+- License/source conflicts are marked “approved” without primary evidence and reviewer identity.
+- Manifest count is the only approval test; no full-set membership/rationale review exists.
