+
+## 3. One hybrid corpus, four distinct product dimensions
+
+A corpus is a **single set of canonical identities**. Classification metadata, pedagogy and discovery are projections over that set—not separate corpora and not additional copies of a character.
+
+| Dimension | Purpose | Evidence required | Role in membership | Role in platform |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Everyday literacy | Practical reading and writing in current Japanese | Exact, approved edition of the official Jōyō/common-use table; source/release and rights pin. KANJIDIC2 grade values may be a cross-check, not the official list itself. | **Recommended strong core inclusion:** all identities in the owner-approved official literacy baseline. | Core learning path, basic reference, writing/reading filters. |
+| Real-world frequency | Improve coverage of Kanji seen in actual Japanese text | A named corpus, date/window, sampling and counting method, license, normalization/tokenization rules, exact per-character frequency/rank. KANJIDIC2 `<freq>` is a verified newspaper ranking but has undocumented survey details in the pinned artifact. | Evidence for extension priority; **not** a sole membership rule. | Ranking/filtering and coverage insights; source-specific rank displayed with caveat. |
+| Learner progression | Coherent education and JLPT-related study | Pinned education standard or clearly sourced level map. KANJIDIC2 `<grade>` and old `<jlpt>` semantics must be preserved; current JLPT list provenance is not established here. | Can support core/extension rationale; JLPT labels are not exclusive membership gates. | Curriculum sequencing, review units and level filters. |
+| Comprehensive reference | Useful name, specialist, historical and advanced reading coverage | Specific official name lists, defined subject/reference sources, dictionary metadata and human-reviewed rationale. Distinguish Japanese-use evidence from mere encoding or dictionary presence. | Evidence-gated extension and documented exceptions; not “everything with a dictionary row.” | Search/reference views, name and specialist facets, advanced lookup. |
+
+### Overlap and separation
+
+- A common Jōyō character may also rank in KANJIDIC2 frequency, have school-grade/JLPT metadata, occur in JMdict compounds, and have a KanjiVG file. It is still **one identity and one membership record** with several evidence labels.
+- An out-of-Jōyō name character may be common in newspaper or name data; the same identity can support frequency and reference objectives. Do not allocate it twice or assign it an invented “name slot” and “frequency slot.”
+- Learning order may differ from stable corpus membership. A character can be in the canonical corpus but scheduled later or only shown in advanced search.
+- Search coverage is the whole approved set. A learner’s current level filters presentation; it does not change corpus membership.
+- A missing dictionary field, decomposition, or stroke asset is a source-coverage status. It is not a zero relevance score and is not automatic exclusion.
+
+## 4. Candidate populations and direct KANJIDIC2 findings
+
+### 4.1 Exact pinned source inspected
+
+**VERIFIED:**
