+## 5. KANJIDIC2 `<freq>` field — direct investigation
+
+### 5.1 Exact field definition
+
+**VERIFIED in the inline DTD of the pinned XML:** the element is `<freq>` under `<misc>`; it is an integer-content frequency-of-use ranking. The DTD says the 2,500 most-used characters are ranked, unranked characters lack the field, values express relative occurrence in modern Japanese, the survey is based on newspapers and is biased toward newspaper articles, discrimination among less frequent characters is weak, and “Actually there are 2,501 kanji ranked as there was a tie.”
+
+- It is a **rank**, not a raw count, continuous score, or category label.
+- Lower numeric rank means more frequent in this ordering: the exact XML has 日 at rank 1, 一 at 2, 国 at 3, 会 at 4, and 人 at 5.
+- Values parsed are integers; each pinned source record had at most one `<freq>` element.
+- The DTD describes the ordinary range as 1–2,500, with a tie producing 2,501 ranked Kanji. The actual pinned XML serializes **all integer values 1–2,501 exactly once**. There are no duplicate rank values, missing rank numbers, non-integers, or values outside 1–2,501 in this XML. Thus the conceptual tie described in the DTD is not represented as two records sharing the same numeric rank; do not infer raw frequency counts or the tied pair from this field.
+
+### 5.2 Measured limitations and suitability
+
+- **VERIFIED:** 2,501 of 13,108 records have the field; 10,607 do not. It therefore identifies a frequency-tagged subset, not a universal character population.
+- **VERIFIED:** 99 of the 2,136 grade-1–6/8 records lack frequency rank; 464 frequency-ranked identities are outside those grades. A frequency-only top set is not the same as a complete Jōyō set.
+- **VERIFIED:** all 82 compatibility ideographs in this source are unranked. Absence does not mean irrelevant; it is missing rank evidence.
+- **VERIFIED from DTD:** newspaper bias and weak discrimination among the lower-frequency entries are acknowledged by the source itself.
+- **UNRESOLVED from artifact:** the precise survey dates, newspaper titles/genres, sample size, extraction method, counting unit and tie calculation are not specified in the pinned file’s field comment. The 2023 XML creation date is not the survey date.
+- **INFERRED:** useful as a reproducible historical newspaper-frequency feature and as one extension ranker; not a modern comprehensive-text benchmark and not an adequate sole inclusion rule.
+
+The “2,501” coincidence is now **directly verified as a count of records with `<freq>` in this source release**. It is still not evidence that the owner’s comprehensive hybrid should equal those 2,501 records.
+
+## 6. Proposed measurable selection methodology
+
+### 6.1 Policy shape
+
+**Recommended policy:** one canonical membership set plus multi-valued, source-backed reasons. Adopt the complete owner-approved official Jōyō table as the strong literacy core, subject to exact edition/pin and source-rights review. Do not use KANJIDIC2 grade as a substitute for the official list; use it as a second source check. Select the extension from a single deduplicated pool of evidence-backed candidates for frequency/real-text use, educational/learner progression, names and specialist/advanced reference.
+
+No four-way numerical allocation is made. A candidate may serve multiple objectives; classification flags are evidence, not allotments. The 365 residual is calculated only after the core source has been frozen and its exact size verified. If the approved core is not 2,136, recompute the residual; do not hard-code 365 as an independently chosen category quota.
+
+### 6.2 Candidate record/evidence evaluation
+
+Evaluate the following as categorical claims with source/version/field references; do not assign invented point weights. The inclusion decision and source-coverage state are separate.
+
+| Evaluation dimension | Valid evidence to record | Membership role | Missing/weak data treatment |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Common-use/literacy relevance | Exact official Jōyō source row/release; separately note school grade. | Core rule if owner approves the official list. | No row or unresolved version → candidate review blocked, not silently inferred from KANJIDIC grade. |
+| Frequency/recent reading use | `<freq>` rank and exact KANJIDIC2 digest; ideally a second named, dated real-text corpus with counting specification. | One extension ordering signal, never sole eligibility. | Null remains `not_ranked`; do not score as zero. Unknown current corpus coverage remains `not_measured`. |
+| Educational/JLPT relevance | `<grade>` raw value and exact DTD semantics; any external JLPT list requires source/license and edition. | Learner label and possible extension evidence; old JLPT not an exclusive gate. | Old level 2 ambiguous across new N2/N3; preserve as `jlpt_old=2`, do not invent split. |
+| Name relevance | Official Jinmeiyō source row/version, `nanori` field, reviewed named-use rationale. | Candidate extension evidence where source and scope are approved. | Missing `nanori` is not proof a Kanji is never used in a name; no fabricated name claim. |
+| Specialist/advanced reading | Named licensed subject corpus, authoritative glossary/standard, source-backed reading/context and reviewer rationale. | Controlled reference extension. | No subject source in current checkout; keep unresolved/quarantined until evidence exists. |
+| Dictionary coverage | KANJIDIC2 exact character record; JMdict `ent_seq`, `k_ele`/written form and coverage method; source pins. | Evidence/facet; dictionary presence is not membership. | Complete JMdict bytes absent; do not claim coverage count. |
+| Kanji metadata coverage | KANJIDIC2 presence and raw per-field states; field/source version. | Quality and product readiness; not sole inclusion. | Distinguish absent, not applicable, not reviewed, unavailable, conflict and quarantined. |
+| Reliable decomposition | First-party edge provenance, reviewed semantic/phonetic claim sources, or explicit KanjiVG structural-only evidence. | Learning usefulness and optional exception support, not identity membership. | Unknown decomposition is not zero relevance; do not turn visual grouping into etymology. |
+| Stroke-order asset | Pinned SVG identity, per-file hash, release, exact path/variant status. | Platform capability only. | `asset_unavailable` is not a membership exclusion unless a separately approved visual-only corpus says so. |
+| Provenance/licensing confidence | Immutable source ID, artifact/version/checksum, field-level source reference, exact notice and approved use. | Hard approval gate for distributable source-derived claims; not a relevance score. | Missing/ambiguous rights → `QUARANTINED`/blocked distribution; never repaired by guessing. |
+
+### 6.3 Deterministic procedure and tie-breaking
+
+1. **Freeze the policy inputs first:** target `2501`; identity class; exact Jōyō/extension source editions; source-use approvals; a versioned, categorical precedence table for extension evidence; Unicode version; variant policy; reviewer roles. These are part of the policy digest.
+2. **Build candidate populations from pinned bytes:** record each candidate as exact literal + one Unicode scalar, raw code point, original source ID/record key and field/value observations. Create a union by exact scalar. Keep source evidence rows separately; do not normalize/deduplicate by glyph or reading.
+3. **Apply the core rule:** add each distinct member of the exact approved Jōyō list once. Compare, do not silently overwrite, with KANJIDIC2 grades. Conflict or unsupported official-list row enters `QUARANTINED` pending review; no reclassification by the grade field alone.
+4. **Build extension evidence labels:** for each out-of-core candidate, assign one or more of `frequency_or_text`, `education_or_learner`, `name`, `specialist_or_advanced`, `dictionary_support`, `metadata_support`, `decomposition_support`, `asset_support`. Labels include source and field, and have explicit confidence/status—not numerical scores.
+5. **Use a frozen categorical precedence, not weights:** first consider the owner-approved “multi-objective evidence” tier (candidate has reviewed evidence for more than one of the four objectives); then the owner-approved single-objective tiers (including name/specialist and frequency/real-text). This precedence is visible in the policy and does not award arbitrary points. Evidence fields from one source are not falsely described as independent sources. **The order among incomparable objective categories remains an owner decision**; if no order is approved, stop in `POLICY_REVIEWED`, do not break the tie by a hidden default.
+6. **Order within a tier deterministically:** for a verified frequency source, smaller rank first; for an official list, use its pinned published ordering if stable, otherwise numeric scalar order; for corpus occurrence counts, descending verified count then source key; for equal/unranked candidates, compare the versioned source-priority key, then numeric code point. The complete tuple and order direction are stored in the policy so a later implementation cannot vary by locale or input-file order.
+7. **Select only the derived remainder:** after core size `c` has passed verification, `r = 2501 − c`. Traverse the frozen extension queue by the approved tuple, selecting each exact identity at most once until `r` distinct extensions have been selected. All categories remain multi-label; one character that supports two objectives consumes one slot.
+8. **Do not pad or silently substitute:** if `c > 2501`, the core/source policy is contradictory; stop. If approved candidates are fewer than `r`, output `BLOCKED` with shortage and source gaps. If disputed candidates straddle the cutoff or an unresolved tie remains, stop for owner review. Never add “next convenient” characters, override missing fields, or remove a first-party baseline row just to hit 2,501.
+9. **Emit a full decision ledger:** for selected, rejected, deferred and quarantined candidates record source evidence, policy rule, reason, reviewer, and cutoff comparison. Produce coverage counts across all four objectives and all candidate sources. If any owner-selected objective has no eligible extension evidence, disclose that gap and require an explicit owner decision before final approval.
+10. **Re-run from pinned inputs:** same policy/source/digest inputs must yield identical sorted membership and decision-ledger hashes. A changed source, policy, identity class, or tie-break creates a new candidate run and requires review.
+
+This is deterministic when its policy table and source inputs are approved. The evidence does **not** justify a neutral total order among frequency, rare-name and specialist candidates. An approved categorical precedence is therefore a required policy input, not an implementation discretion.
+
+### 6.4 Concrete policy alternatives for the extension conflict
+
+| Alternative | Deterministic rule | Benefits | Limitations / owner decision |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **A — Frequency-priority extension** | If official core verifies at 2,136, select 365 lowest-rank KANJIDIC2 `<freq>` identities outside it. Ranks are unique in this pin. | Exact, simple, reproducible; the extension size is derived, no invented four-way quotas. | Would choose frequency over name/specialist candidates without ranks; 99 unranked Jōyō are protected only by the separately sourced core. Newspaper ranking is old/bias-limited and its license gate is unresolved. Does not by itself substantiate the “comprehensive reference” goal. **Alternative, not recommended as full hybrid.** |
+| **B — Coverage-aware hybrid extension (recommended)** | Keep complete literacy core; create multi-tag evidence rows from pinned frequency/text, education/learner, name and specialist sources; use reviewed multi-objective tier then a published categorical precedence and stable within-source ordering. | Best fit to all four objectives; a character can support several views once; no arbitrary point weights or category quotas. | Requires source pins, owner-approved precedence, and license review; the exact selection cannot be simulated safely until these exist. |
+| **C — Editor-selected exception set** | Human panel selects extension candidates using documented reasons; numeric scalar order only for final serialization. | Can add genuinely useful rare/name/specialist items missed by old rankings. | Least reproducible if reviewers use free-form discretion; requires item-level citations, two-person review and explicit approval for every exception. Use only within a published rule, not to fill a shortfall. |
