+## 7. Stable Unicode identity and variant policy
+
+### 7.1 Canonical identity recommendation
+
+**Recommended default:** one canonical identity equals one exact, eligible Unicode scalar value, represented by both its literal scalar and a derived `U+` code point. The owner must approve the eligible Unicode/script class (including whether all CJK Unified Ideographs, compatibility ideographs and special ideographic numerals are eligible). Unicode properties do not decide Japanese educational usefulness.
+
+- Canonical ID derives from exact code point, e.g. `kanji:u+<hex>`; do not base identity on glyph, reading, meaning, database row ID or normalized text.
+- No NFC or NFKC normalization in manifest identity, source evidence or uniqueness checks. Preserve exact source scalar and raw original. NFC/NFKC may be used only in a separate derived search key with a reversible source-to-key relation and collision report.
+- Count one scalar once. Reject unpaired UTF-16 surrogates, overlong/invalid source encodings, multi-scalar strings, invalid/out-of-range Unicode values and mismatched literal/code point.
+- Supplementary-plane ideographs are not excluded merely because older local `kanjiText.ts` extraction code only covers BMP ranges; the identity validator must use full Unicode scalar iteration and the approved Unicode property table/version.
+
+### 7.2 Four non-interchangeable kinds of relation
+
+| Type | Example/meaning | Identity/count rule | Search/display rule |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Separate canonical character identity | Distinct code points for orthographic/historical forms; compatibility character U+FA10 and a related unified form U+585A are a known example pair. This example is not a membership decision. | Keep as distinct identities if both pass policy; never collapse merely for similar appearance or compatibility mapping. | Exact input wins; show a typed, sourced relation and permit deliberate navigation. |
+| Glyph/display variant | Font, regional glyph design, KanjiVG file variant or presentation style of the same Unicode scalar. | Not an additional member. Store asset/glyph metadata against the base identity. | Render chosen glyph with source/font context; do not rewrite canonical text. |
+| Compatibility/alias mapping | Unicode compatibility/canonical mapping or curated alternate input spelling. | Relation, not a replacement. Record direction, mapping source and whether mapping is Unicode-defined or editorial. Alias is not counted again. | Exact match first; alias lookup may return a related identity. If ambiguous, present choices; never silently replace stored identity. |
+| Editorial/historical/orthographic relationship | Shinjitai/kyūjitai, traditional/simplified relation, historical form, semantic/phonetic relation. | Distinct only when Unicode identities differ; typed relationship is not proof of equivalence or shared history. | Facet/link with evidence and reviewer status. Do not merge many-to-many or context-dependent relations. |
+
+**Variation sequences (IVS/VS):** recommended default is the base ideograph is one membership identity. A registered ideographic variation sequence is glyph/presentation metadata keyed by base+selector and is not a second Kanji. Store sequence source and registration status; never strip it from source evidence silently. Owner approval is needed if the product instead counts sequences.
+
+**Multiple readings/forms:** readings, okurigana, nanori and vocabulary readings attach to the exact character/lexical entry with source type. They do not create additional member identities. A word reading must not be mislabeled as a character reading.
+
+### 7.3 Deterministic duplicate and alias validation
+
+- Require exactly one Unicode scalar and check literal ↔ `U+` equality.
+- Duplicate identity check is exact integer code point across the complete manifest. Same scalar from multiple sources produces one member and multiple evidence links.
+- Distinct code points never deduplicate by NFC, NFKC, visual rendering, radical, reading, meaning, or transliteration.
+- Build a separate alias/relation table keyed by source and exact target code points. Validate that targets exist, relation types are allowed and any many-to-many mapping is explicit.
+- Search resolves exact scalar first, then approved aliases; preserves user input and canonical target in separate fields; emits all ambiguous related identities rather than choosing by source order.
+- Report normalized-key collisions before release; never use a collision to reduce the 2,501 count.
