+### 11.2 Serialization, ordering and immutability
+
+- Recommended serialization: canonical JSON per a pinned RFC 8785/JCS implementation or versioned repository-owned canonicalizer, UTF-8 without BOM, LF line endings. Record the canonicalizer implementation/version. Do not normalize strings during serialization.
+- `entries` order: numeric Unicode scalar ascending, with `ordinal` derived from that order. Do not use locale sort. Source evidence arrays and relationships also receive fixed tuple sorts.
+- Validate the parsed object before hashing. SHA-256 covers canonical bytes of all frozen manifest content except the `manifestSha256` property itself; it includes policy/source locks, entries, counts, state and fixed timestamps. Detached approval record signs/references the final digest to avoid circular hashes.
+- Freeze the manifest and ledger read-only after all approvals. Any membership, policy, source pin, identity or approval-state change creates a new `corpusVersion`, digest, `supersedes` link, and deterministic old/new delta. Never edit an approved manifest in place.
+- A source-refresh-only change gets new source and derived-evidence identities; reviewers determine whether membership policy inputs changed. Preserve every prior frozen artifact and approval record.
+- A later batch names the exact `manifestId`, `corpusVersion`, `manifestSha256`, policy digest, source-lock digest, member IDs and transformation version. Batch boundaries do not create different corpora.
